Subject: Make mypyc's CPyPtr a real pointer under Fil-C
Origin: Filnix
Upstream-Status: Not submitted

mypyc emits code like `r = *(CPyPtr *)((CPyPtr)list + offsetof(PyListObject,
ob_item)); *(PyObject **)r = item;` with CPyPtr being size_t. Fil-C drops the
capability when a pointer is loaded from memory as an integer, so the store
traps ("cannot write pointer with null object"), e.g. in charset-normalizer's
compiled cd.mb_encoding_languages. Only Fil-C sees the new typedef.
Regression check: python312Packages.charset-normalizer's test suite.

--- a/mypyc/lib-rt/mypyc_util.h	2026-09-27 11:53:57.624702758 +0000
+++ b/mypyc/lib-rt/mypyc_util.h	2026-09-27 11:54:07.012759999 +0000
@@ -120,7 +120,16 @@
 // have the tag bit set.
 typedef size_t CPyTagged;
 
+#ifdef __FILC__
+// Generated code loads pointer fields (such as PyListObject.ob_item) as
+// CPyPtr and then writes through them. Fil-C only keeps a pointer's
+// capability when it travels through memory as a pointer, so an integer
+// CPyPtr would lose it and the write would trap. A char pointer supports
+// the same offset arithmetic and casts.
+typedef char *CPyPtr;
+#else
 typedef size_t CPyPtr;
+#endif
 
 #define CPY_INT_BITS (CHAR_BIT * sizeof(CPyTagged))
 
